course: adult-life
lesson: 55
title: Favourite films and series: spoilers, binge-watching, streaming and adverts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Films and series are one of the great joys of family life: favourite films watched ten times, movie nights, crying at sad endings, cheering at happy ones. Grown-ups love them as much as children do.
explain: But screens are also a business. Streaming services cost money every month and are built to keep you watching; adverts are made to grab you and make you want things; and age ratings exist because some films are made for older people.
explain: Vivid example: in a living room in Brisbane it is movie night. Freya wants the dragon film; her brother wants the football one; they shout. Mum pins a rota on the fridge: "Freya this Friday, Sam next Friday. Sorted. Now, who's making the popcorn?"
explain: Good habits make screens better, not worse: take turns, don't spoil endings, turn off autoplay, keep screens out of bedrooms at night, and remember that a day without screens makes room for other fun.
fact: Rewatching a favourite film is a comfort at any age; grown-ups do it too.
fact: A family rota, where each person chooses the film in turn, makes movie night fair.
fact: Films often change a book's story to fit the screen and the time; the film is a new telling and the book is still there.
fact: A spoiler tells the ending before someone has seen it; it is annoying, and spoiling on purpose is a small meanness that needs a "sorry".
fact: Streaming plays the next episode automatically to keep you watching; turning off autoplay helps you decide when to stop.
fact: Binge-watching a whole series in one weekend, with little sleep and no fresh air, can make you feel tired and strange the next day.
fact: Adverts are made to stand out, often louder and faster than the programme, and toys in adverts are shown with close-ups and tricks that make them look bigger and better.
fact: Food adverts aimed at children have rules about what they can show; a snack does not make a show better.
fact: Each streaming service costs money every month and has different shows; families choose which ones are worth paying for.
fact: Crying at a sad film or when a favourite character dies is normal and shows you care; sad endings can make a story feel real.
fact: Age ratings tell families who a film is made for; in the UK a 12A film can be seen in a cinema by a younger child with an adult, after the parent decides it is right.
fact: Screens in bed keep you awake, so many families keep screens downstairs at night.
fact: Liking a film has nothing to do with being a boy or a girl, or with your age; you are never too old for something you enjoy.
fact: Anyone can make a film with a phone and some toys, and the credits at the end thank the hundreds of people who made a big film.
exercise: Charlie still cheers at his favourite superhero film on the tenth watch. Is he too old for it? | No; rewatching favourites is a comfort at any age | Yes; you should only ever watch a film once, then move on | Yes; superhero films are only made for little kids | No, but he must stop after the tenth time
exercise: Freya and her brother fight every movie night over which film to watch. What makes it fair? | A rota, so each person chooses in turn | The loudest person in the family always chooses the film | Whoever is the oldest always chooses the film | Nobody in the family watches anything ever again
exercise: The film of Alfie's favourite book changes the ending. What does Dad say? | Films and books are different tellings, and the book is still there | The film-makers ruined the story on purpose just to annoy readers like him | The book's ending is now wrong forever | He must never read the book again
exercise: Grace's friend tells her the ending of a new film. What is a sensible reaction? | It's annoying, but not worth ending a friendship over | Never speak to the friend again, not even at school | Spoil every single film for the friend in return, to be fair | Refuse ever to watch the film now
exercise: Jake spoils the end of a series for his sister on purpose to win an argument. What should he do? | Say sorry, because spoiling on purpose is unkind | Nothing, because it's a funny and clever way to win | Spoil another series to show he is right | Tell her the ending was a lie
exercise: Ruby asks for "just one more episode" for the fifth time. What helps? | Turning off autoplay and one episode on school nights | Watching all night until she is too tired to keep her eyes open | Letting the app decide when she stops | Hiding the remote from Mum and Dad
exercise: Harry watched a whole series in one weekend and feels strange and tired on Monday. Is he ill? | No; he needs sleep, a walk and less screen | Yes; he has caught a series virus from the TV | Yes; he must stay off school all week | No; he needs to watch another series
exercise: Poppy notices the adverts are louder than her show. Is the TV broken? | No; adverts are made to grab your attention | Yes; the TV must be repaired today | Yes; the volume button is stuck | No; the programme got quieter by itself
exercise: An advert shows a toy that looks huge and moves by itself. Max gets it and it's small and stiff. Why? | Adverts use close-ups and tricks to show toys at their best | The shop sold him a broken toy on purpose | The toy shrank in the post | Max must be playing with it wrongly
exercise: A junk food advert comes on in the middle of Sophie's favourite show. Does she need the snack to enjoy the show? | No; adverts tell you what to want, and you can choose | Yes; the show is only fun if you eat that snack while you watch | Yes; the show will stop if she doesn't buy it | No, because snacks are banned during shows
exercise: Theo's family pays for three streaming services. Why not just one? | Each has different shows, and each costs money | One service gets them all for free if you ask nicely | The TV only works with three of them plugged in | It is the law to have three services
exercise: Lily cries when the dog dies at the end of the film and is embarrassed. What does Mum say? | Crying at a sad film is normal and shows she cares | Only babies cry at films about dogs, so she should stop | She should hide in her room to cry | Dogs in films never really die so stop
exercise: Oscar's favourite character dies in the last series. He thinks the writer is cruel. What does Dad say? | Sad endings can make a story feel real; it's fine to grieve | The writer is cruel and should be punished for killing the character | Characters always come back if you wait | He should never watch a series again
exercise: All Evie's friends have seen a 12A film and she is nine. What is the sensible thing? | Mum checks the film and decides, and goes with her | Watch it secretly at a friend's house, so nobody gets upset | Ratings are meaningless, so just go | She can never see it until she is twelve
exercise: Finn wants to watch a series on his phone in bed. What does Dad decide? | Screens stay downstairs at night | Only with the sound turned right up | Only after midnight, when it's quiet | Yes, as long as he watches cartoons
exercise: Ava's family has a "screen-free Sunday" and she is bored. What is it? | A break to do other fun things together | A punishment for something she did wrong | A day when the TV is away being repaired | A rule that lasts for the whole of the year
exercise: Noah's friend says real men don't watch romantic films, so Noah pretends he hated one. What is true? | Anyone can like any film, boy or girl | Boys are not allowed to like romantic films | Only grown-up women may watch romantic films | Liking a romantic film makes you less of a boy
exercise: Charlie thinks the credits are boring and wants to skip them. Why are they there? | To thank the hundreds of people who made the film | To give the cinema staff time to sweep the floor before the next show | To show the names of everyone who watched | To make the film long enough to sell
test: Leo, first time at the cinema, grabs Dad's hand at the loud bit: "it's SO loud!!" What does Dad say that helps? | It's the big sound system; you can cover your ears | Close your eyes tight until the film ends | Shout back louder to beat it | Leave and never come to a cinema again, ever
test: Mia secretly loves her little brother's cartoon: "don't tell anyone, it's for babies." What would Mum say? | You're never too old for something you enjoy | She is right; she should stop watching it at once | Only babies are allowed to laugh at cartoons | Watching it will make her act like a baby
test: Isla: "Can't make a film, we're not rich, you need like a million pounds." What does Dad do? | Helps her make a short film with a phone and toys | Agrees and puts the phone away | Says films are only made in America | Tells her to wait until she's a grown-up
test: Big brother, as the adverts start: "Oi, they've cranked the volume again." Why are adverts often louder? | To grab your attention so you notice the product | Because the TV turns itself up at night to wake you | Because the programme ran out of sound | Because the adverts are filmed outdoors
test: Dad, looking at the bank app: "Three streaming things? Bloody hell, we never watch that one." What is he most likely to do? | Cancel the one they don't use | Pay for a fourth one just in case | Ring the bank to complain about films | Watch all three at once to get his money's worth
test: Ruby: "I'm not even tired, I could watch till morning, honest." It's 10 pm on a school night. What is really going on? | Autoplay keeps her going, and she'll be tired tomorrow | She has found the secret to never sleeping, and feels great | The series will be deleted in the morning | She is right; screens make you less tired, so it's fine
test: Grace's friend blurts out the ending. Grace: "I'm never talking to you again!!" A week later she watched it and loved it anyway. What's the lesson? | Spoilers are annoying, but friendship is worth more | Spoilers make films impossible to ever enjoy | She should get her own back by spoiling | Never talk to anyone before a film
test: Evie's mate: "My mum let me watch a 15, so ratings are rubbish." What are ratings really for? | To guide families about who a film is made for | To stop all children from ever watching TV or films | To tell you how good a film is out of 15 | To make films cost more at the cinema
test: Freya, cross: "Why is it ALWAYS his turn?!" Mum points to the fridge. What's on it? | A rota showing whose turn it is | A list of films Freya isn't allowed | A drawing of their favourite film | The rules of the big cinema in town
test: Little brother, about the dog dying in the film: "Lily's crying, what a baby lol." Put him right. | Crying at a sad film is normal and shows you care | He's right, only babies cry at films about dogs | Lily is crying because she is hungry | It's fine to laugh at people who cry
test: Max shows Mum a new advert: "It flies round the room by itself!!" What does Mum suggest before buying? | Look at real reviews, because adverts show toys at their best | Buy two, in case one flies away out of the window | Believe it, because adverts must show the truth | Wait for the toy to fly to the house
test: Harry's mate: "Watched the whole series in two days, buzzing, didn't even go outside!" On Monday he's grumpy and tired. Why? | Too much screen, little sleep and no fresh air | The series put some kind of spell on him | He watched it on the wrong channel | Mondays are always tiring for everyone, whatever they do
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the automatic playing of successive episodes upon streaming services. | The prolongation of the time spent viewing | The protection of the viewer's eyesight | The reduction of the monthly subscription | The prevention of spoilers among friends
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning age ratings of films. | They guide families as to the audience for which a film is intended | They indicate the quality of a film on a scale of one to eighteen | They prohibit all persons under eighteen from viewing any film whatsoever | They determine the price of admission to the cinema
exam: Whereas a toy appears in an advertisement larger and more animated than it proves in reality, determine the explanation most probably applicable. | The advertisement employs close-ups and visual techniques | The toy has diminished in size during the course of delivery | The retailer has supplied a defective toy deliberately | The purchaser has failed to operate the toy correctly
exam: Ascertain the appropriate characterisation of the deliberate disclosure of a film's ending to a person who has not yet viewed it. | A minor unkindness for which an apology is due | A criminal offence punishable by the courts | A customary courtesy between brothers and sisters | A service which renders the film more enjoyable
